  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 115 PM EDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis/Forecast: Low pressure that crossed lower MI last \nnight is now situated well downstream in far southern Quebec/just \nnorth of upstate NY. Pressure gradient has tightened significantly \nthis morning between that departing low and high pressure to our \nwest -- this'll remain the case through this evening. Parent trough \naxis to the ongoing late week inclement weather is in the process of \ncrossing the western Great Lakes this afternoon and early evening, \nwith mid-upper level heights rising in its wake for the weekend.  By \nSaturday afternoon through Sunday, northern MI will lie on the \nnorthern periphery of ~1032mb sfc high pressure centered to our \nsouth. Meanwhile, low pressure is expected to be crossing well to \nour north across parts of northern Saskatchewan/Manitoba/Ontario. A \ntightening pressure gradient locally will be the result once again, \nwith renewed breezy to windy conditions. High pressure largely \nexpected to remain in control all the way through the middle of next \nweek before shower chances return toward the very tail end of the \nforecast period as a northern stream system looks to pass by to our \nnorth. \n\nForecast Details: Stronger dynamics are in the process of \nshifting south and east out of northern MI this afternoon, \nlargely taking any synoptically driven rain showers along with \nit. However, strong cold advection supporting increased overlake\ninstability on the surrounding Great Lakes will likely keep at \nleast scattered shower activity across parts of the forecast \narea into this evening (most numerous through sunset in the \ntypical NNW flow lake belts). Still a non-zero chance that a few\nwet snowflakes mix in after sunset, primarily across the \ntypically colder higher terrain/ interior areas of north-central\nlower MI -- that's if these areas can materialize a few \nlingering showers, something that's not favored given northerly \nlow-level winds. Otherwise, windy conditions remain through this\nevening as well with gusts as high as 25-40 mph area-wide \n(highest near the lakeshores). Winds relax mid-evening through \nearly Saturday morning.\n\nMore sunshine expected for the weekend ahead, but breezy to windy \nconditions expected to return by Saturday afternoon lasting through \nthe day on Sunday as northern MI becomes sandwiched between high \npressure centered to our south and low pressure crossing well to the \nnorth. Winds largely out of the southwest through this time frame, \ngusting as high as 20-30 mph on Saturday, and 25-35+ mph at times on \nSunday. Daytime highs on Saturday in the low-mid 50s moderate on \nSunday -- largely ranging from the upper 50s far north to the low-\nmid 60s across much of northern lower.\n\nThis warming trend from Sunday continues into at least the middle of \nnext week as Pacific-dominant flow pattern should allow temperatures \nto remain above normal for mid-October. Highs Monday through \nWednesday largely in the 60s area-wide, but a few spots climbing \ninto the low 70s seem pretty likely. Next rain chances look to hold \noff until Thursday as a northern stream wave is progged to pass by \nto our north. Doesn't appear this will be a big rain maker, but may \nput a damper on temperatures. Even so, no glaring signals for a \nreturn of below or even near normal temperatures through the end of \nnext week.",
